---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Project">
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-12 pb-16">
    <p class="font-mono text-sm text-orbit tracking-widest mb-4" data-reveal="1">
      ONE-PAGER
    </p>
    <h1 data-reveal="1">AI Agent for In-Space Assembly</h1>

    <h2 data-reveal="2">The problem</h2>
    <p data-reveal="2">
      <!-- TODO: State the problem from your field's point of view. Be ambitious.
      What is hard about assembling large structures in orbit today? -->
      Placeholder: assembling large structures (trusses, telescopes, habitats) in
      orbit requires long, error-sensitive sequences of robotic operations that
      are costly to plan and verify. This section frames that challenge.
    </p>

    <h2 data-reveal="3">Why it matters</h2>
    <p data-reveal="3">
      <!-- TODO: Why does solving this matter to your field and beyond? -->
      Placeholder: robust autonomous assembly unlocks larger apertures, cheaper
      infrastructure, and missions that cannot be launched fully assembled.
    </p>

    <h2 data-reveal="4">What I expect the agent to do</h2>
    <p data-reveal="4">
      <!-- TODO: Describe how you imagine using an agent: what it reads, writes,
      runs, or checks. You don't need to know how yet. -->
      Placeholder: the agent will read assembly specifications and constraints,
      draft a step-by-step assembly plan, run it against a simulator or checker,
      detect constraint violations, and iterate toward a feasible sequence.
    </p>

    <h2 data-reveal="5">Data and restrictions</h2>
    <p data-reveal="5">
      <!-- TODO: Note any data sources and confirm nothing proprietary/sensitive. -->
      Placeholder: all data used will be public, synthetic, or redacted — no
      proprietary or sensitive material, per class policy.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
